(2865833, 395988, 20530, '<td valign="top">Congrats on your top 10 Aimee! This is a wonderful and humorous shot. I love the expressions and animation and especially appreciate how you did it in-camera. The white plate adds nice contrast to the dark BG. As always, your creativity and talent with fruit shines through!</td>', 'Congrats on your top 10 Aimee! This is a wonderful and humorous shot. I love the expressions and animation and especially appreciate how you did it in-camera. The white plate adds nice contrast to the dark BG. As always, your creativity and talent with fruit shines through!', '2006-09-26 12:10:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2865839, 394201, 20530, '<td valign="top">Great work on this shot! Definitely underrated. I like it even more, knowing the story of your cooperative neighbors. Photography sure can make one do some funny stuff, huh? Have they asked for a framed print for their wall, yet?</td>', 'Great work on this shot! Definitely underrated. I like it even more, knowing the story of your cooperative neighbors. Photography sure can make one do some funny stuff, huh? Have they asked for a framed print for their wall, yet?', '2006-09-26 12:13:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2866833, 395988, 30982, '<td valign="top">Aimee - AWESOME score and great finish!  I can only dream of scores in that range and you just sort of rack ''em up!  Good job on beating the hubby, too.</td>', 'Aimee - AWESOME score and great finish!  I can only dream of scores in that range and you just sort of rack ''em up!  Good job on beating the hubby, too.', '2006-09-26 18:51:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2866909, 395961, 17507, '<td valign="top">I love this picture! The cat''s expression is perfect, the "cat audience" is charming, and the humor is right on the mark.</td>', 'I love this picture! The cat''s expression is perfect, the "cat audience" is charming, and the humor is right on the mark.', '2006-09-26 19:50:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(2867010, 394685, 53147, '<td valign="top">Well - you got hit hard on this one. I had a feeling this was yours when you were posting your score in the threads. I give you credit for attempting some pretty intense postprocessing, although I guess I wasnt really sure what you were shooting for. I too didnt get the tie in to Larson, but then I didnt get all of his work either. I think your editing just sent this over the top into way abstract land and it couldnt find its way back. I would be interested in hearing your vision and concept though. That might bring a bit of clarity into the work.</td>', 'Well - you got hit hard on this one. I had a feeling this was yours when you were posting your score in the threads. I give you credit for attempting some pretty intense postprocessing, although I guess I wasnt really sure what you were shooting for. I too didnt get the tie in to Larson, but then I didnt get all of his work either. I think your editing just sent this over the top into way abstract land and it couldnt find its way back. I would be interested in hearing your vision and concept though. That might bring a bit of clarity into the work.', '2006-09-26 20:50:31.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(2867151, 394685, 11854, '<td valign="top">Hey Tim, <br/> You asked so I''ll tell... what I was going for with the processing was a cartoon look, since FarSide is a cartoon. I went to the zoo specifically to shoot this and the tree for the Lone Tree challenge. 2 birds and all that. I had two ideas for this challenge (the other being what I posted for an outtake, which was my first idea, this was the fall back). Larson used a lot of animals, and a lot of the times they were getting the best of the humans. That''s what I was going for here. I wanted it to appear that the frog swallowed the child in retaliation for messing with it''s children (the eggs behind it). I didn''t want to copy an exact cartoon (of which I have a whole book of his). I''m finding I never do well with original ideas here though.<br/> <br/>edit to add: the frog actually had a big hole in the back for the kids to climb into, I just filled it in with Photoshop. <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-09-26 21:15:41.</i></td>', 'Hey Tim,  You asked so I''ll tell... what I was going for with the processing was a cartoon look, since FarSide is a cartoon. I went to the zoo specifically to shoot this and the tree for the Lone Tree challenge. 2 birds and all that. I had two ideas for this challenge (the other being what I posted for an outtake, which was my first idea, this was the fall back). Larson used a lot of animals, and a lot of the times they were getting the best of the humans. That''s what I was going for here. I wanted it to appear that the frog swallowed the child in retaliation for messing with it''s children (the eggs behind it). I didn''t want to copy an exact cartoon (of which I have a whole book of his). I''m finding I never do well with original ideas here though. edit to add: the frog actually had a big hole in the back for the kids to climb into, I just filled it in with Photoshop. ', '2006-09-26 22:13:05.000000', '2006-09-26 22:15:41.000000', 0),
(2867711, 395777, 28742, '<td valign="top">Congrats on this, one of my absolute favorites in the challenge!</td>', 'Congrats on this, one of my absolute favorites in the challenge!', '2006-09-27 01:24:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2869534, 396019, 61679, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/>For your first challenge entry, you achieved a very commendable score.  I knew the competition for this challenge would be tough as soon as it was announced and you were up to the challenge.  My first impression of this entry is that the image itself does not cry Gary Larson on its own.  However, you came up with a very strong caption that was easy to relate to Gary Larson.  Nice job.<br/> <br/>On the technical side, the tree trunk on the right side of the image adds nothing to the composition and could have been cropped out.  This would have made your image close to square, which is a trait of Gary Larson cartoons as well.  I also agree with the commenter that the focus on the caterpillar looks a little soft (not sharply focused).  This could be a result of camera movement, using the macro mode on your camera (which produces a very shallow depth of focus), or forgetting to sharpen the image after resizing for the challenge.  If you did use macro mode, you might want to try a different mode next time (like portrait) to get some extra depth of focus.  However, you probably won''t be able to focus as closely and will need to crop more.  If it is related to camera movement, it a tripod would help.  <br/> <br/>You might want to play around with levels and saturation to see if you can give the colors a bit more pop.  All in all, you made me laugh, which was the intent of your entry.  <br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me if you have any questions regarding this critique.<br/> <br/>Tim<br/></td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club, For your first challenge entry, you achieved a very commendable score.  I knew the competition for this challenge would be tough as soon as it was announced and you were up to the challenge.  My first impression of this entry is that the image itself does not cry Gary Larson on its own.  However, you came up with a very strong caption that was easy to relate to Gary Larson.  Nice job. On the technical side, the tree trunk on the right side of the image adds nothing to the composition and could have been cropped out.  This would have made your image close to square, which is a trait of Gary Larson cartoons as well.  I also agree with the commenter that the focus on the caterpillar looks a little soft (not sharply focused).  This could be a result of camera movement, using the macro mode on your camera (which produces a very shallow depth of focus), or forgetting to sharpen the image after resizing for the challenge.  If you did use macro mode, you might want to try a different mode next time (like portrait) to get some extra depth of focus.  However, you probably won''t be able to focus as closely and will need to crop more.  If it is related to camera movement, it a tripod would help.   You might want to play around with levels and saturation to see if you can give the colors a bit more pop.  All in all, you made me laugh, which was the intent of your entry.   Feel free to PM me if you have any questions regarding this critique. Tim', '2006-09-27 15:15:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(2869657, 394099, 66254, '<td valign="top">Absolutely amazing work!  Congrats.</td>', 'Absolutely amazing work!  Congrats.', '2006-09-27 15:49:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(2869699, 396070, 16912, '<td valign="top"><b>Thoughts from Ecce, with his critique club hat on</b> <br/>Hi Rodney and congratulations on your highest scoring image (thus far), it will take some work to keep your average above 5.8 but I''m sure you''ll do it ;) To be fair I can''t really critique this as a photograpgh (the picture of the dog is just too small)so I will give my thoughts as a challenge entry.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>You said it was last minute but just a little more time to position the dog more evenly would have improved this greatly imo.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Once again, difficult to comment as there are no real technical things to comment on (other than positioning of copies)<br/> <br/><b>Aesthetics, Challenge effectiveness</b> <br/>To be honest although I''ve seen his cartoons before I never realised they were Gary Larson cartoons and I must admit this entire challenge was fun to view. Yes, it fits the challenge well and yes, I had a good chuckle (you could easily replace the dog with all manner of subjects). Overall its a fun ''poster'' that closely follows the remit and worthy of its top 20 finish (probably would have been higher if it was better positioned).<br/> <br/>Well done and looking at <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=392252" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/59877/thumb/392252.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/59877/thumb/392252.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/59877/thumb/392252.jpg"/></a>  you have a great subject to shoot :) I''d call this one menacing!</td>', 'Thoughts from Ecce, with his critique club hat on Hi Rodney and congratulations on your highest scoring image (thus far), it will take some work to keep your average above 5.8 but I''m sure you''ll do it ;) To be fair I can''t really critique this as a photograpgh (the picture of the dog is just too small)so I will give my thoughts as a challenge entry. Composition You said it was last minute but just a little more time to position the dog more evenly would have improved this greatly imo. Technical Once again, difficult to comment as there are no real technical things to comment on (other than positioning of copies) Aesthetics, Challenge effectiveness To be honest although I''ve seen his cartoons before I never realised they were Gary Larson cartoons and I must admit this entire challenge was fun to view. Yes, it fits the challenge well and yes, I had a good chuckle (you could easily replace the dog with all manner of subjects). Overall its a fun ''poster'' that closely follows the remit and worthy of its top 20 finish (probably would have been higher if it was better positioned). Well done and looking at   you have a great subject to shoot :) I''d call this one menacing!', '2006-09-27 16:02:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(2869972, 394300, 31133, '<td valign="top">Hey, what''s up with your score here?! THis is a great shot and it looks to me like you put a lot of work into it. Great facial expression!! (I also like the fact that the cat looks satisfied that it''s safe to leave you alone for a few days, what with that fancy feeder and all ;-)</td>', 'Hey, what''s up with your score here?! THis is a great shot and it looks to me like you put a lot of work into it. Great facial expression!! (I also like the fact that the cat looks satisfied that it''s safe to leave you alone for a few days, what with that fancy feeder and all ;-)', '2006-09-27 17:47:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(2870487, 394907, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Nice photo, and technically very well done.  Good exposure, very good action capture.<br/> <br/>I think what hurt you here is that it was not obvious enough as to what what going on.  The title tells us that he is "falling up", which to me works for the far side.  I''m beginning to think I remember on about falling up as opposed to falling down.  Maybe it''s just my imagination.<br/> <br/>However, the subject isn''t obviously falling up.  I think the key word is obvious.  Most voters spend much less than a minute per image.  The story has to be told either qucikly or in a dramatic fashion so as to hold th interest untill the teller if finished.  He looks like he is doing a flip, or falling down.  His body language head positon, all are responding to normal gravity.  So though the title says falling up, to many voters saw falling down, and some may never have even read the title.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Nice photo, and technically very well done.  Good exposure, very good action capture. I think what hurt you here is that it was not obvious enough as to what what going on.  The title tells us that he is "falling up", which to me works for the far side.  I''m beginning to think I remember on about falling up as opposed to falling down.  Maybe it''s just my imagination. However, the subject isn''t obviously falling up.  I think the key word is obvious.  Most voters spend much less than a minute per image.  The story has to be told either qucikly or in a dramatic fashion so as to hold th interest untill the teller if finished.  He looks like he is doing a flip, or falling down.  His body language head positon, all are responding to normal gravity.  So though the title says falling up, to many voters saw falling down, and some may never have even read the title.', '2006-09-27 21:20:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(2870529, 395777, 62009, '<td valign="top"><b>Greets from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Congrats on your yellow.  Seems that I''m usually lucky (or unlucky) enough to critique a lot of ribbon winners.  <br/> <br/>This is a great take on Larson.  Done superbly.  The colors in this are great.  Amazing expression on his face.  <br/> <br/>My only suggestion would be to use less Neat Image.  The skin looks a bit plasticy.  I think what saved that part is the fact that this is a cartoon repro.<br/> <br/>I really enjoyed this photo.<br/> <br/>Congrats again.<br/> <br/>Mike<br/></td>', 'Greets from the Critique Club  Congrats on your yellow.  Seems that I''m usually lucky (or unlucky) enough to critique a lot of ribbon winners.   This is a great take on Larson.  Done superbly.  The colors in this are great.  Amazing expression on his face.   My only suggestion would be to use less Neat Image.  The skin looks a bit plasticy.  I think what saved that part is the fact that this is a cartoon repro. I really enjoyed this photo. Congrats again. Mike', '2006-09-27 21:33:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2870552, 395979, 62009, '<td valign="top"><b>Greets from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>This is a great Larson-esque title and fits the photo quite well.<br/> <br/>I think this would have done much better if there wasn''t as much PP.  Jar Jar looks like he was drawn onto the wood background and not a figurine.  I think if he would have been really crisp and clear, this would have done better.<br/> <br/>Also, the fact that this is obviously not a traditional Larson, probably hurt the voting.<br/> <br/>Very imaginitive take.<br/> <br/>MIke</td>', 'Greets from the Critique Club  This is a great Larson-esque title and fits the photo quite well. I think this would have done much better if there wasn''t as much PP.  Jar Jar looks like he was drawn onto the wood background and not a figurine.  I think if he would have been really crisp and clear, this would have done better. Also, the fact that this is obviously not a traditional Larson, probably hurt the voting. Very imaginitive take. MIke', '2006-09-27 21:38:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2871001, 394143, 34255, '<td valign="top">great pic...and your cat is very pretty and big</td>', 'great pic...and your cat is very pretty and big', '2006-09-27 23:57:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2871282, 396007, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>I think you are already aware of the biggest problem.  It is hard to see exactly what is going on with the camera.  It it sideways, upsdide down, lens cap on, backwards, what?  <br/> <br/>The flowers intrude in the camera area, and distract the eye, the details of the camera are hidden in shadow.  I don''t believe the dark clothing really affected the camera detail, the clothing just blends into the shadow that obscures the needed information about the camera.  <br/> <br/>Other than that, focus is good, depth of field is OK.  The closest flowers are soft, but hard focus is not really needed there.  Skin tones do look a little bit cool.  Flowers are a little under saturated for my taste.<br/> <br/>Overall an interesting and funny photo.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: I think you are already aware of the biggest problem.  It is hard to see exactly what is going on with the camera.  It it sideways, upsdide down, lens cap on, backwards, what?   The flowers intrude in the camera area, and distract the eye, the details of the camera are hidden in shadow.  I don''t believe the dark clothing really affected the camera detail, the clothing just blends into the shadow that obscures the needed information about the camera.   Other than that, focus is good, depth of field is OK.  The closest flowers are soft, but hard focus is not really needed there.  Skin tones do look a little bit cool.  Flowers are a little under saturated for my taste. Overall an interesting and funny photo.', '2006-09-28 01:33:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(2872286, 395903, 32885, '<td valign="top">Nice one. I like the colour. The backgound can be a bit of a distraction but you worked with what you had. Good job.</td>', 'Nice one. I like the colour. The backgound can be a bit of a distraction but you worked with what you had. Good job.', '2006-09-28 10:59:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2872769, 396046, 62009, '<td valign="top"><b>Greets from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>This is a fairly comical photo, however, I think the caption draws away from it.  It''s quite long and not very Larson-esque.  I know this was a Larson tribute, but it seems the shots that recreated one of his works did a lot better than the ones that were tributes to his style.<br/> <br/>The rock head seems a bit out of focus around it''s mouth area.  The James Brown head is quite dark. And there are a lot of reflections in the boy character''s face.<br/> <br/>It was a good take and the composition is pretty good.<br/> <br/>I think it would have scored higher if it was a recreation rather than a tribute shot.<br/> <br/>Mike</td>', 'Greets from the Critique Club  This is a fairly comical photo, however, I think the caption draws away from it.  It''s quite long and not very Larson-esque.  I know this was a Larson tribute, but it seems the shots that recreated one of his works did a lot better than the ones that were tributes to his style. The rock head seems a bit out of focus around it''s mouth area.  The James Brown head is quite dark. And there are a lot of reflections in the boy character''s face. It was a good take and the composition is pretty good. I think it would have scored higher if it was a recreation rather than a tribute shot. Mike', '2006-09-28 13:39:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2873272, 395979, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by cryingdragon:</b></div><hr/><i>I think this would have done much better if there wasn''t as much PP.  Jar Jar looks like he was drawn onto the wood background and not a figurine.  I think if he would have been really crisp and clear, this would have done better.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks for the comments!<br/> <br/>Since this was a tribute to a cartoon, I felt it appropriate to try and give it a cartoony appearance, especially after I saw how bad the original looked (see below). I also wanted to add an element of unreality by having the shrimp obviously real.<br/> <br/>If I had had more time available I would have looked for a better background than the wood, but I was quite rushed when I took this, I think in the last hour before submission time. I was originally going to do this with a T rex, but I couldn''t find my son''s toy dinosaur collection in time; Jar Jar was a compromise.<br/> <br/>Resized, RGB Curve, USM:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=402860" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/1031/thumb/402860.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/1031/thumb/402860.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/1031/thumb/402860.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Originally posted by cryingdragon:I think this would have done much better if there wasn''t as much PP.  Jar Jar looks like he was drawn onto the wood background and not a figurine.  I think if he would have been really crisp and clear, this would have done better. Thanks for the comments! Since this was a tribute to a cartoon, I felt it appropriate to try and give it a cartoony appearance, especially after I saw how bad the original looked (see below). I also wanted to add an element of unreality by having the shrimp obviously real. If I had had more time available I would have looked for a better background than the wood, but I was quite rushed when I took this, I think in the last hour before submission time. I was originally going to do this with a T rex, but I couldn''t find my son''s toy dinosaur collection in time; Jar Jar was a compromise. Resized, RGB Curve, USM:', '2006-09-28 16:59:38.000000', NULL, 0),
